okay so let's get down to the business  end where we are going to face the main  line of the colorado gambit which start  after move bishop to b5  and here i recommend that you should  certainly capture this pawn and after  knight to e5 we should play queen to d6  and we already know what you should do  after queen to e2 so the main line runs  with the move d4  and here i recommend that you should  play knight to f6 and this is pretty  much the starting position of the main  line  now if you can see i have highlighted by  the arrows over here white has few good  options  but before we go and look at those  options let me tell you something  from last one year or so i am trying  colorado gambit against different rated  players  and  even earlier i was a bit scared of the  main line but from my heavy research  from the last six months  i am now confident enough to play this  line against anybody  there are a few things you should note  before we continue  black king will remain in the center for  a while but  fight cannot exploit this and  meanwhile black can create certain kind  of imbalance which will confuse the  whole wide setup  the amount of complexity exists in this  line it's even  very complex for the higher level up to  iem or gm level and that's why it's very  unlikely that your opponent is going to  churn out let's say 10 15 moves of the  computer in today's world where most of  the chess are playing with the clock you  can also get heavy benefit of the time  margin  so considering all this let's get  started so the first move which i want  to consider castle  and here i recommend that you should  play immediately knight to d7 so idea is  very simple black one to capture this  knight and  according to this thread  white has the only two good reply which  are the f4 and bishop to f4 so let's see  each by turn so first move i want to  consider is bishop to f4 afterwards  black has to capture this knight with  the c knight and here again white has  two choices if bishop captures e5 then  black is going to play queen to b6  attacking the bishop and wide response  is ready for pretty much force in one of  my game my opponent decided to play  knight to c3 but after very simple c6  the resulting position is very much  favorable to the black the further game  goes like this so my opponent plays  bishop to d3 but after captures captures  and  again captures captures i played the  move g6  and after knight to a4 attacking my  queen i simply attack his knight  and after b3 i played bishop to g7  attacking e5 and my upon responded with  the move f4 where i responded with the  move e6 and here my opponent responded  with queen to e3 so his threat is very  simple he wants to play knight to c5 but  it's time to pause over here what black  his achieve is  black has managed to  exchange his bad bishop and put his all  pawns on the light square and black has  this wonderful dark square bishop as you  will see as the game goes on this will  become more and more  plus position for the black so here i  want to stop knight to c5 with any sort  of concession so i drop my bishop back  to f8 and here my opponent attacked my  queen with the move a3 i dropped my  queen back to the a5 and now my opponent  played rook to f3 and here i played  bishop to e7 and you can see the  flexibility of this opening  any moment blackened castle not only on  king side but black can also go on queen  side and here my opponent plays knight  to c3 and this is what happened when you  play without any plan and the simple  repetition is bishop to c5 and gaining  the queen and the game  so this game illustrate a very simple  fundamental how you should play if you  opponent play knight to c3 supporting  that bishop  instead of knight to c3 at this point  the most logical reply from the white  will be capturing that knight so bishop  cross d7 and here i recommend that you  should capture with the king  and  do not afraid because your king is quite  safe and white cannot exploit it  immediately  so let's see some of my games from here  onward so my opponent responded with  knight to c3 and i played queen to c6  and here my opponent play queen to f3 so  attacking the bishop attacking the pawn  so  black response is pretty much forced  black has to play e6 and now my opponent  responded with queen to g3 so his idea  is very simple it's not only  pressurizing the c7 but it's also  pressurizing the g7 but right now g7 is  not a not a big threat because if the  bishop takes d7 then the rook can come  to the g8 and white will lose a piece so  that's why i calmly played rook to d8  and now i can do a artificial castle  with king to c8 and king goes to the b8  and black position is very very fun  let's see how the game progress so my  opponent responded here with knight to  e2 and i played king to c8 and after  knight to f4 i played rook to b8 so my  planning is very simple i want to play  the move g5 and hit the knight so my  opponent stop that with the move h4  and here i played bishop to e7 renewing  that threat and my opponent responded  with the rook to even which is a  slightly inaccuracy because now comes  the tactics which is  bishop takes h4 and after queen takes in  g5  my opponent is pointed with queen to g3  but after very simple knight takes queen  takes bishop to e4 i'm attacking the  juicy g2 square here my opponent made a  mistake he thought that he can cover  this g2 with the move f3  but after very simple rook to x8  that pawn is a goner so  in the game it happens like this so  after queen to h4  bishop takes f3 and g3 so stopping that  threat but after queen to c2  we don't need to go further from here we  can already see  black is quite fine and has a winning  position  so that was about bishop to f4 let's  consider the another move which is the  move f4 and here again we are going to  capture this with the d knight so knight  d cross  e5 please note white cannot capture this  knight with the d pawn otherwise queen  to c5  leads to  the bishop will be a goner so that's why  f cross e5 is pretty much force and  after f cross e5 i recommend that you  should play queen to g6  so attacking the c2  and not only that there are in some  lines this bishop can also  launch onto the h3 this game my opponent  responded with bishop to a4 protecting  the c2 and now i played the move e6 free  up the other bishop and after knight to  c3 bishop to e7  and bishop to e3  i played castle on the king side and  after knight to b5 attacking the c7 pawn  probably this is the weakest part in  this variation but this can be met very  very tactically so i played a very very  cunning move itself which is knight to  e5  and here my opponent fall into this trap  so my opponent captured this c7  afterwards i first of all play a knight  to c4 attacking the bishop so y doesn't  have time to capture this rook so bishop  drop backs to the f2 and now i hit this  knight one more time with rook to c8 and  after knight to b4  can you spot it yeah it's knight cross  b2  and white will lose a piece  so that is one tactical way if you open  and place knight to b4  and that is pretty much you need to know  when your opponent plays bishop to f4  and f4 this is  most of the  likely response you are going to face  you

